WHAT THIS REPOSITORY IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is the server-side half of ToolsEnabled's connectivity: a rendezvous relay
that verifies signed short leases, pairs exactly two endpoints, and forwards
sealed frames it cannot open. It is published so that a person who wants to run
their own can read every line of what they would be running.

It is NOT the account service. The relay reads the account service's device
records read-only, over the local filesystem, and holds no accounts, no billing
and no customer content of its own. Deploying this repository does not give you
the hosted service; it gives you the part a self-hoster needs.

The client half -- everything that runs on a customer's own machine -- is
licensed the same way and is released from the ToolsEnabled engine repository.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CRYPTOGRAPHY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This software uses cryptography solely through platform primitives -- Node.js
`node:crypto` and, at the browser edge, WebCrypto. It implements no cryptographic
algorithm of its own and contains no homegrown construction. Its one third-party
dependency, the websocket library, performs no cryptography beyond the
SHA-1 handshake digest RFC 6455 mandates.

The primitives it calls are:

  - Ed25519 signature verification, over lease material and over the nonce a
    browser signs to prove possession of its key.
  - SHA-256, for lease and key fingerprints.
  - X.509 certificate parsing, to read the fingerprint of a client certificate
    presented at the TLS edge. Parsing only: this software issues no
    certificate and signs nothing.
  - Constant-time comparison, and random generation for nonces.

It forwards frames sealed by its endpoints and holds no key that can open them.

